{
  "statement": "The safetyist argues that a mandatory five-year retention window preserves institutional memory and prevents labs from erasing safety tests during rapid updates. The accelerationist contends that multi-year static record archives are technologically obsolete and create defensive legal compliance exercises that chill internal discourse. Both positions assume that document retention alone determines whether corporate safety practices are honest or obscured. But the five-year retention window is an empty compliance ritual unless paired with mandatory external auditing and whistleblower protections.\n\nCorporate safety documentation functions primarily as legal risk management and corporate self-protection rather than transparent public disclosure. Historical parallels in corporate crisis management show that firms archive internal memos strictly to limit liability rather than inform the public. When an engineering team flags a flaw in a model version released in June 2024, that memo is filed away to manage future lawsuits, not to protect users. Without structural transparency, storing a file on a server for sixty months simply extends the statute of limitations for paper trails while keeping the contents locked behind trade secret protections.\n\nStatic retention rules do nothing to pierce the evidentiary dead ends created by trade secrets, protective orders, and proprietary weight protection. Legal discovery patterns in corporate litigation involving algorithmic systems show that plaintiffs and external auditors are walled off by non-disclosure agreements even when records exist. A lab can retain a safety report for the full five years while successfully blocking access in court through standard protective orders. Mandating storage without mandating public access or independent inspection transforms safety logs into confidential assets protected by the very companies that built the system.\n\nCritics will argue that without this rule, labs will immediately destroy inconvenient safety reports the moment a model update ships. This objection misses the core mechanical failure of the proposal. The primary barrier to safety accountability is not document preservation. The barrier is the total lack of enforcement mechanisms to compel external inspection of those documents by regulators or injured parties.",
